                <pb facs="0831" n="733" sameAs="frus:frus1930v02/pg733"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">836.48 1930 Hurricane/32: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in the Dominican Republic</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Curtis</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santo Domingo</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1930-09-09T12:00:00-04:40">September 9,
                        1930—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received 1:35 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>104. My 103 September 9, 11 a.m.<anchor n="18" xml:id="d847-f1"/> Dominican Army
                    and police are almost completely demoralized and the moral effect of having 50
                    marines here would be enormously beneficial. Major W. B. Sullivan who has acted
                    as Navy Department liaison officer states that Trujillo requests me through him
                    to have such detachment sent here. Major Sullivan particularly recommends
                    sending also Captain Thomas E. Watson, United States Marine Corps.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Curtis</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
